// _tests_/bit/fmtcitations/fmtcitationsmd.bit — the whole `<path>.md:NNN`
// citation-drift arm (#3675): extraction and the docs-document checks, plus
// (since #4707) the SOURCE-comment half of the same arm —
// checkSourceMdCitation/checkSourceMdQuote, called from scanSourceFile in
// fmtcitationssrc.bit — and this arm's own four self-tests
// (selfTestMdCitations, selfTestMdHeadingAnnotation, selfTestMdRangeCitations,
// selfTestCitableContent), which fmtcitationsselftest.bit's selfTest() calls
// as siblings. Sibling in the same directory module as fmtcitations.bit,
// so everything here calls straight into its Scan/report/die/trimWs/isWs/
// isIdentStartCh/isIdentContCh/docLineFor/parseIntSimple with no import,
// per docs/development.md's "Splitting a file" convention. Wired in from
// fmtcitations.bit's main() via checkMdCitations — this extends the
// existing `./make test-fmt-citations` gate; it is not a new Gate{}.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS: docs/development/provider-duplication.md cited line 708
// of docs/development.md twice; the real content had drifted to line 781 on
// both sides. Every stale line number named in this file is written as
// "line N of <file>", never as a `<file>:N` citation — it is a specimen of
// a citation, not one, and writing it in citation shape would hand the
// scanner below a target it is REPORTING as wrong (line 708 is blank today,
// which the #4670 content check fails by design). (git archaeology: both
// citations named the SAME line when
// the file was written in 05453a89, because that line was then the
// "Runtime core and OS providers" heading — the section grew beneath it,
// the heading and its content moved down together, and nothing ever
// re-checked the numbers). fmtcitations.bit's own scanner only recognizes
// `.bit:NNN` tokens (see its extractPathTokens, hardcoded to bitSuffix), so
// a `.md:NNN` citation could drift forever with the gate staying green.
//
// SCOPE, stated explicitly rather than implied by this file's name (a gate
// whose name suggests full coverage while it covers two files is a
// documented failure mode in this repo): every `.md` file under `docs/`,
// walked recursively (buildMdIndex) — 53 files as of #3675. NOT spec/**,
// NOT runtime/ABI.md, NOT README.md/CONTRIBUTING.md: `grep -rnoE
// '[A-Za-z0-9_./-]+\.md:[0-9]+'` across those trees plus docs/ at write time
// found exactly THREE `.md:NNN`-shaped citations in the whole repository,
// all three under docs/ (two in provider-duplication.md, one in
// docs/lint/runtime-policy.md citing spec/LINT.md:242) — so docs/ is the
// entire live problem, not an arbitrary slice of it. Re-grep before trusting
// that stays true; if a citation appears elsewhere, widen buildMdIndex's
// walk root rather than adding a second scanner.
//
// THAT GREP WAS RIGHT ABOUT `.md` DOCUMENTS AND WRONG ABOUT `.bit` SOURCE
// (#4653): 47 source comments under compiler/, tools/build/ and _tests_/bit/
// carry a document citation with a line number, and none of them was
// bounds-checked by anything until fmtcitationssrc.bit's own arm
// (checkSourceMdCitation) started feeding them through checkBoundsShared.
// extractMdCitations below is what that arm calls to find them, so this
// file's scanner is now shared by two populations — the docs/ walk here,
// and every `//` comment fmtcitationssrc.bit already reads.
//
// TWO TIERS, deliberately unequal in strength, to avoid the false-positive
// trap a markdown citation invites (a `.md:NNN` reference can legitimately
// name a heading or a section rather than one exact sentence, and a gate
// demanding sentence-level exactness on prose gets disabled within a week):
//
//  BOUNDS (every citation, via checkBoundsShared): the cited file exists
//  under the repo root and every line number the spec names — both ends of
//  a range, #4668 — falls in [1, its current line count]. Catches a citation
//  that moved past EOF; does NOT catch one that drifted to a different but
//  still in-range line.
//
//  CONTENT (every citation, via lineHasCitableContent, #4670): the cited
//  line is neither blank nor a code-fence marker. The cheapest half of the
//  in-range drift class, and it catches the instance that motivated the
//  ticket — compiler/arm64compile.bit:83 named line 1160 of spec/SPEC.md, a
//  blank line, for a sentence that is at spec/SPEC.md:1414 today. Two stale
//  citations in the live corpus were repointed for it: line 233 of
//  spec/LINT.md is blank and its sentence is at spec/LINT.md:242.
//
//  TWO PREDICATES FOR ONE ANNOTATION READER, and the split is measured
//  rather than stylistic. #4670 proposed running HEADING over source
//  comments as well: the only source comment carrying the annotation shape
//  then was compiler/lexinvisible.bit:96, and its parenthetical quotes the
//  cited SENTENCE, not a heading — the heading predicate would have failed
//  one site out of a population of one. A document names a section; a
//  source comment quotes prose. So headingAnnotationAfter is shared and the
//  verifier is not: lineIsHeadingContaining for docs/**.md here,
//  lineContainsQuoted for `//` comments (fmtcitationssrc.bit's
//  checkSourceMdQuote, #4677). The live failure that check was built on:
//  five comments cited line 68 of spec/SPEC.md for the byte order mark
//  sentence, which sits at spec/SPEC.md:70 — bounds passed all five (68 is
//  in range) and so did CONTENT (line 68 is an ordinary bullet about
//  modules), and only the quoted text says which line was meant.
//
//  HEADING (opt-in per citation, via headingAnnotationAfter +
//  lineIsHeadingContaining): a citation shaped `` `path.md:NNN`
//  ("Some Heading Text") `` — the citation immediately followed (within
//  maxAnnotationGap bytes, crossing at most one markdown line-wrap and one
//  closing backtick) by a parenthetical double-quoted string — is checked
//  precisely: line NNN in the target must itself be a markdown heading
//  (trimmed, starts with "#") whose text contains the quoted string. A bare
//  citation with no such annotation gets bounds only; inventing a nearby
//  heading to check it against would be exactly the guessing this file's own
//  scope note warns against.
//
// Denominators are always printed by mdSummaryLine (files scanned, citations
// found, bounds-checked, content-checked, heading-verified, failures) so this
// cannot regress
// into scanning zero files while reporting a clean pass — the same
// discipline fmtcitations.bit's own summary line already applies to
// `.bit:NNN` citations, and to the source arm's quote-verified count, which
// is printed there because that arm accumulates into the headline Scan.

const mdSuffix: string = ".md"
const mdMarker: string = ".md:"
const maxMdFiles: int = 256
const maxMdBytes: int = 1048576
const maxMdCitations: int = 128
const maxAnnotationGap: int = 8
const maxHeadingAnnotationLen: int = 96
const minMdCitationsFloor: int = 2

class MdCitation {
  file: string,
  lineSpecRaw: string,
  docLine: int,
  startOffset: int,
  endOffset: int,
}

// Every `.md` file under `<root>/docs`, repo-relative, bounded by maxMdFiles.
fn buildMdIndex(root: string): []string {
  let walkArg = "docs"
  let strip = ""
  if (root != ".") {
    walkArg = "${root}/docs"
    strip = "${root}/"
  }
  let all = walk(walkArg) catch e {
    die("cannot walk ${walkArg}: ${e.message()}")
    []string(0)
  }
  if (len(all) > maxMdFiles) {
    die("${walkArg} holds ${len(all)} entries, over the maxMdFiles bound of ${maxMdFiles}")
  }
  let out = []string(0)
  for p of all {
    if (!hasSuffix(p, mdSuffix)) {
      continue
    }
    let canon = p
    if (len(strip) > 0 && hasPrefix(p, strip)) {
      canon = p[len(strip):len(p)]
    }
    out = append(out, canon)
  }
  return out
}

// The next ".md:" occurrence in `text` at or after `i`, or -1.
fn findMdMarker(text: string, i: int): int {
  let relRaw = indexOf(text[i:len(text)], mdMarker)
  if (relRaw < 0) {
    return -1
  }
  return i + int(relRaw)
}

// Scans backward from `markerAt` over identifier/slash characters for the
// start of the path token before ".md:" (bounded by maxTokenLen). Returns
// `markerAt` itself (a no-op scan) if nothing path-like precedes it.
fn scanMdPathStart(text: string, markerAt: int): int {
  let start = markerAt
  let floor = clampFloor(markerAt - maxTokenLen)
  while (start > floor && (isIdentContCh(text[start - 1]) || text[start - 1] == '/')) {
    start = start - 1
  }
  return start
}

// The end of a `NNN[-MMM][, NNN[-MMM]...]` line spec starting at `startAt`,
// bounded by maxLineSpecLen. Accepts the same `isLineSpecChar` set
// scanPathTokenSpecEnd (fmtcitationsextract.bit) accepts, so the end of a
// RANGE reaches parseSegments instead of being truncated at the '-' — until
// #4668 this scanned digits only, so `runtime/ABI.md:283-290` was checked at
// 283 and the "-290" was never looked at, and a range whose end had moved
// past EOF passed forever.
//
// `isLineSpecChar` admits ' ' and ',', so the scan runs on into ordinary
// prose after a citation ("spec/LINT.md:186, E0204)" — twelve live sites,
// "spec/SPEC.md:70 -- The byte order mark" — one). Backing off every
// trailing non-digit is what keeps those out: a line spec always ends in a
// digit, so the trailing "` `", "`, `" or "` -- `" is dropped and the
// returned offset lands one past the last digit, exactly where the
// digits-only scan used to land. That matters beyond parsing — `endOffset`
// is where headingAnnotationAfter starts looking.
fn scanMdLineSpecEnd(text: string, startAt: int): int {
  let n = len(text)
  let q = startAt
  while (q < n && q - startAt < maxLineSpecLen && isLineSpecChar(text[q])) {
    q = q + 1
  }
  while (q > startAt && !isDigitCh(text[q - 1])) {
    q = q - 1
  }
  return q
}

// Every `<path>.md:NNN[-MMM]` citation in `text`, via findMdMarker/
// scanMdPathStart/scanMdLineSpecEnd above. Simpler than extractPathTokens:
// no bare-mention tracking and no requiredPrefix, because this gate only
// checks citations that name an exact line.
fn extractMdCitations(text: string): []MdCitation {
  let out = []MdCitation(0)
  let n = len(text)
  let i = 0
  while (i < n && len(out) < maxMdCitations) {
    let markerAt = findMdMarker(text, i)
    if (markerAt < 0) {
      break
    }
    let afterMarker = markerAt + len(mdMarker)
    let start = scanMdPathStart(text, markerAt)
    if (start == markerAt || !isIdentStartCh(text[start])) {
      i = afterMarker
      continue
    }
    if (afterMarker >= n || !isDigitCh(text[afterMarker])) {
      i = afterMarker
      continue
    }
    let q = scanMdLineSpecEnd(text, afterMarker)
    out = append(
      out,
      MdCitation{
        file: text[start:markerAt] + mdSuffix, lineSpecRaw: text[afterMarker:q],
        docLine: docLineFor(text, start), startOffset: start, endOffset: q,
      },
    )
    i = q
  }
  return out
}

// Skips one optional backtick then any whitespace, bounded by `limit`.
fn skipMdAnnotationLeadIn(text: string, startAt: int, limit: int): int {
  let i = startAt
  if (i < limit && text[i] == '`') {
    i = i + 1
  }
  while (i < limit && isWs(text[i])) {
    i = i + 1
  }
  return i
}

// The double-quoted string starting at `text[i]` (`i` must sit on the
// opening '"'), up to the next '"' within maxHeadingAnnotationLen bytes —
// or "", false if `i` is not on a quote or the string never closes.
fn readMdQuoted(text: string, i: int): (string, bool) {
  let n = len(text)
  if (i >= n || text[i] != '"') {
    return "", false
  }
  let start = i + 1
  let j = start
  while (j < n && text[j] != '"' && j - start < maxHeadingAnnotationLen) {
    j = j + 1
  }
  if (j >= n || text[j] != '"') {
    return "", false
  }
  return text[start:j], true
}

// The quoted string in a `("...")` annotation immediately after
// `afterOffset` (within maxAnnotationGap bytes, allowing one optional
// closing backtick and whitespace/newline between them) — or "", false.
fn headingAnnotationAfter(text: string, afterOffset: int): (string, bool) {
  let n = len(text)
  let limit = afterOffset + maxAnnotationGap
  if (limit > n) {
    limit = n
  }
  let i = skipMdAnnotationLeadIn(text, afterOffset, limit)
  if (i >= limit || text[i] != '(') {
    return "", false
  }
  return readMdQuoted(text, i + 1)
}

// Whether `lines[lineNum-1]`, trimmed, is a markdown heading ("#" prefix)
// whose text contains `needle`.
fn lineIsHeadingContaining(lines: []string, lineNum: int, needle: string): bool {
  if (lineNum < 1 || lineNum > len(lines)) {
    return false
  }
  let line = trimWs(lines[lineNum - 1])
  if (len(line) == 0 || line[0] != '#') {
    return false
  }
  return contains(line, needle)
}

// Whether `lines[lineNum-1]` contains `needle` anywhere — the SOURCE-comment
// arm's annotation predicate (#4677), deliberately weaker than
// lineIsHeadingContaining above: a document names a SECTION, so its
// annotation must land on a heading, while a source comment quotes the
// PROSE it was written against, which is an ordinary line. Same annotation
// reader (headingAnnotationAfter), two predicates, one per convention —
// measured on the live corpus, not assumed: the heading predicate failed the
// only annotated source comment there was.
//
// Not trimmed and not case-folded: the quote is copied from the cited line,
// so an exact substring is what a correct annotation produces, and anything
// looser starts accepting a quote the line does not actually carry. An EMPTY
// quote is refused rather than passed: `contains(line, "")` is true for every
// line, so counting it would put a vacuous annotation into the verified
// population.
fn lineContainsQuoted(lines: []string, lineNum: int, needle: string): bool {
  if (lineNum < 1 || lineNum > len(lines)) {
    return false
  }
  if (len(needle) == 0) {
    return false
  }
  return contains(lines[lineNum - 1], needle)
}

// The first line number `spec` names (a range's start), or 0 when it does
// not parse — the line a heading annotation is checked against now that a
// citation may name a range (#4668). Only reached after checkBoundsShared
// has already accepted the spec, so the 0 is a guard, not a live path.
fn specFirstLine(spec: string): int {
  let segs = parseSegments(spec)
  if (len(segs) == 0 || !segs[0].ok) {
    return 0
  }
  return segs[0].start
}

// Whether `lines[lineNum-1]` carries content a citation can be pointing at:
// not blank, and not a fenced-code delimiter. This is the cheapest check
// that separates a citation from one that has DRIFTED to a different but
// still in-range line, which bounds passes forever — the measured instance
// (#4670) is compiler/arm64compile.bit:83, which named line 1160 of
// spec/SPEC.md for "only promises the register is excluded from the
// allocator": that line is blank, inside the traits section, and the
// sentence lives at spec/SPEC.md:1414 today. 1160 < 4747, so nothing ever
// objected.
//
// It is a floor, not the whole class — a citation that drifts onto another
// paragraph still passes, and the two designs that would catch that were
// measured and rejected here. See this file's HEADING note.
fn lineHasCitableContent(lines: []string, lineNum: int): bool {
  if (lineNum < 1 || lineNum > len(lines)) {
    return false
  }
  let line = trimWs(lines[lineNum - 1])
  if (len(line) == 0) {
    return false
  }
  return !hasPrefix(line, "```") && !hasPrefix(line, "~~~")
}

// BOUNDS always; HEADING when `c` is annotated. Bounds runs through
// fmtcitations.bit's checkBoundsShared — the one Tier-1 implementation the
// three DocRow documents and the source-comment arm already share, so a
// range citation is checked at BOTH ends here for free (#4668) instead of
// through a second, digits-only parse of its own. `sc` accumulates
// citations/boundsChecked/verified/failures exactly like fmtcitations.bit's
// own Scan use for `.bit:NNN` citations — same fields, a separate instance
// so the two families print separately (mdSummaryLine).
fn checkOneMdCitation(sc: Scan, root: string, docPath: string, docText: string, c: MdCitation) {
  sc.citations = sc.citations + 1
  let res = checkBoundsShared(
    sc,
    root,
    BoundsQuery{
      docPath: docPath, docLine: c.docLine, resolved: c.file,
      parseSpec: c.lineSpecRaw, displaySpec: c.lineSpecRaw, verb: "cites",
    },
  )
  if (!res.ok) {
    return
  }
  sc.boundsChecked = sc.boundsChecked + 1
  let lineNum = specFirstLine(c.lineSpecRaw)
  if (!lineHasCitableContent(res.lines, lineNum)) {
    report(
      sc,
      "${docPath}:${c.docLine}: cites ${c.file}:${lineNum}, but that line is blank or a code-fence marker — the citation has drifted",
    )
    return
  }
  sc.contentChecked = sc.contentChecked + 1
  let (needle, hasAnnotation) = headingAnnotationAfter(docText, c.endOffset)
  if (!hasAnnotation) {
    return
  }
  if (!lineIsHeadingContaining(res.lines, lineNum, needle)) {
    report(
      sc,
      "${docPath}:${c.docLine}: cites ${c.file}:${lineNum} as (\"${needle}\"), but that line is not a heading containing that text",
    )
    return
  }
  sc.verified = sc.verified + 1
}

// Scans every `.md` file under docs/ for `.md:NNN` citations into `sc`.
// Returns the number of files scanned so the caller can print it as the
// scope denominator alongside sc's own citation/failure counts.
fn checkMdCitations(sc: Scan, root: string): int {
  let files = buildMdIndex(root)
  for docPath of files {
    let full = "${root}/${docPath}"
    let docText = readFile(full) catch e {
      die("cannot read ${full}: ${e.message()}")
      ""
    }
    if (len(docText) > maxMdBytes) {
      die("${full} is ${len(docText)} bytes, over the ${maxMdBytes} bound")
    }
    for c of extractMdCitations(docText) {
      checkOneMdCitation(sc, root, docPath, docText, c)
    }
  }
  if (sc.citations < minMdCitationsFloor) {
    report(
      sc,
      "only ${sc.citations} .md:NNN citation(s) found across ${len(files)} docs/**.md file(s), want >= ${minMdCitationsFloor} — the scanner regressed or every citation was removed",
    )
  }
  return len(files)
}

// A zeroed Scan for checkMdCitations' own bookkeeping — kept here so
// fmtcitations.bit's main() stays a one-line call instead of repeating this
// module's field list a second time.
fn newScan(): Scan {
  return Scan{
    citations: 0, boundsChecked: 0, contentChecked: 0, verified: 0,
    quoteVerified: 0, possessivePaired: 0, bareMentions: 0, hedges: 0,
    hedgesChecked: 0, failures: 0,
  }
}

// The "docs/**.md: ..." segment fmtcitations.bit's main() appends to its
// final summary line — kept here so the scope statement (docs/**.md, not
// the whole repo) lives beside the code that enforces it.
fn mdSummaryLine(sc: Scan, files: int): string {
  return "docs/**.md: ${files} file(s) scanned, ${sc.citations} citation(s) (${sc.boundsChecked} bounds-checked, ${sc.contentChecked} content-checked, ${sc.verified} heading-verified), ${sc.failures} failure(s)"
}

// The QUOTE check (#4677) for one source-comment citation, run after bounds
// and content have both passed: a citation followed by a `("...")`
// annotation must name a line that CONTAINS the quoted text.
//
// Same annotation reader as the docs arm (headingAnnotationAfter), different
// predicate — lineContainsQuoted, not lineIsHeadingContaining. #4670
// proposed the heading predicate here and measured it wrong: the whole
// population of annotated source comments was one site, whose parenthetical
// quotes the cited SENTENCE, and a heading predicate fails it. A document
// names a section; a source comment quotes prose.
//
// Opt-in per citation, exactly as the docs arm's annotation is: a bare
// citation gets bounds and content only. That is the point of the shape —
// the writer who quotes the sentence is asking for the stronger check, and
// inventing a quote for an unannotated citation would be the guessing this
// module refuses everywhere else.
fn checkSourceMdQuote(fc: SourceFileCtx, blockText: string, q: SourceQuoteQuery) {
  let (raw, hasAnnotation) = headingAnnotationAfter(blockText, q.endOffset)
  if (!hasAnnotation) {
    return
  }
  let needle = unwrapCommentQuote(raw)
  if (!lineContainsQuoted(q.lines, q.lineNum, needle)) {
    report(
      fc.sc,
      "${fc.path}:${q.actualLine}: cites ${q.file}:${q.lineNum} as (\"${needle}\"), but that line does not contain that text — the citation has drifted or the quote is wrong",
    )
    return
  }
  fc.sc.quoteVerified = fc.sc.quoteVerified + 1
}

// The five values checkSourceMdQuote needs about one citation, bundled so it
// stays inside E0202's 5-parameter limit — the same reason SourceFileCtx and
// CheckCtx exist.
class SourceQuoteQuery {
  file: string, lineNum: int, actualLine: int, endOffset: int, lines: []string,
}

// Tier 1 for one `<doc>.md:NNN` citation in a source comment: the document
// exists at the path the comment names, every line number the spec names is
// within [1, its line count], and the FIRST of those lines carries content.
// `checkBoundsShared` and `lineHasCitableContent` are the same two the three
// DocRow documents' own citations go through, so a source comment and a
// document get one implementation of each, not two.
//
// The content check (#4670) is the cheapest thing that separates a live
// citation from one that has drifted onto a still-in-range line, which
// bounds passes forever. It is a floor: a drift onto another paragraph
// still passes — which is what the QUOTE check above (#4677) narrows, for
// the citations that opt into it. The measured instance it was built on:
// five comments cited spec/SPEC.md:68 for the byte order mark sentence,
// which is at spec/SPEC.md:70; bounds passed all five and so did content,
// because line 68 is an ordinary bullet about something else.
fn checkSourceMdCitation(
  fc: SourceFileCtx,
  block: CommentBlock,
  c: MdCitation,
  dbPositions: []int,
) {
  if (isInsideDoubleBacktickSpan(dbPositions, c.startOffset)) {
    return
  }
  let actualLine = block.startLine + c.docLine - 1
  fc.sc.citations = fc.sc.citations + 1
  let res = checkBoundsShared(
    fc.sc,
    fc.root,
    BoundsQuery{
      docPath: fc.path, docLine: actualLine, resolved: c.file,
      parseSpec: c.lineSpecRaw, displaySpec: c.lineSpecRaw, verb: "cites",
    },
  )
  if (!res.ok) {
    return
  }
  fc.sc.boundsChecked = fc.sc.boundsChecked + 1
  let lineNum = specFirstLine(c.lineSpecRaw)
  if (!lineHasCitableContent(res.lines, lineNum)) {
    report(
      fc.sc,
      "${fc.path}:${actualLine}: cites ${c.file}:${lineNum}, but that line is blank or a code-fence marker — the citation has drifted",
    )
    return
  }
  fc.sc.contentChecked = fc.sc.contentChecked + 1
  checkSourceMdQuote(
    fc,
    block.text,
    SourceQuoteQuery{
      file: c.file, lineNum: lineNum, actualLine: actualLine,
      endOffset: c.endOffset, lines: res.lines,
    },
  )
}

// #3675: extractMdCitations (fmtcitationsmd.bit) must find a `.md:NNN`
// token, skip a bare `.md` mention with no line spec, and never mistake a
// glob for one — the same three shapes selfTestPathTokens already checks
// for `.bit:NNN`.
fn selfTestMdCitations(): int {
  let failed = 0
  let text = "See `docs/development.md:781` and bare docs/development.md mention, then `spec/LINT.md:233` too."
  let cites = extractMdCitations(text)
  failed = failed + selfCheck(len(cites) == 2, "did not find exactly 2 .md:NNN citations")
  if (len(cites) == 2) {
    failed = failed +
      selfCheck(
        cites[0].file == "docs/development.md" && cites[0].lineSpecRaw == "781",
        "misparsed the first .md citation",
      )
    failed = failed +
      selfCheck(
        cites[1].file == "spec/LINT.md" && cites[1].lineSpecRaw == "233",
        "misparsed the second .md citation",
      )
  }
  failed = failed +
    selfCheck(
      len(extractMdCitations("matches under `docs/**/*.md` and nothing else")) == 0,
      "wrongly extracted a token from a glob pattern",
    )
  return failed
}

// #3675: headingAnnotationAfter must find a `("...")` annotation right after
// a citation (crossing one line-wrap, per provider-duplication.md's own
// style), and must NOT invent one out of unrelated later prose — the exact
// shape of provider-duplication.md's two real citations, one annotated and
// one bare.
fn selfTestMdHeadingAnnotation(): int {
  let failed = 0
  let t1 = "the epic. `docs/development.md:781`\n(\"Runtime core and OS providers\") states the rule"
  let c1 = extractMdCitations(t1)
  failed = failed + selfCheck(len(c1) == 1, "did not find the annotated citation")
  if (len(c1) == 1) {
    let (needle1, ok1) = headingAnnotationAfter(t1, c1[0].endOffset)
    failed = failed +
      selfCheck(
        ok1 && needle1 == "Runtime core and OS providers",
        "did not read the heading annotation across the line-wrap",
      )
  }
  let t2 = "still cannot see two things a green build also misses (`docs/development.md:781`): emitted"
  let c2 = extractMdCitations(t2)
  failed = failed + selfCheck(len(c2) == 1, "did not find the bare citation")
  if (len(c2) == 1) {
    let (_, ok2) = headingAnnotationAfter(t2, c2[0].endOffset)
    failed = failed + selfCheck(!ok2, "invented a heading annotation for a bare citation")
  }
  let lines = ["intro", "## Runtime core and OS providers", "body"]
  failed = failed +
    selfCheck(
      lineIsHeadingContaining(lines, 2, "Runtime core and OS providers"),
      "did not match a real heading",
    )
  failed = failed +
    selfCheck(
      !lineIsHeadingContaining(lines, 1, "Runtime core and OS providers"),
      "matched a non-heading line",
    )
  failed = failed +
    selfCheck(
      !lineIsHeadingContaining(lines, 2, "Something else"),
      "matched a heading with unrelated needle text",
    )
  return failed
}

// #4668: extractMdCitations stopped at the '-' of a range, so
// `runtime/ABI.md:283-290` was bounds-checked at 283 only and an end past
// EOF passed. The spec scan now takes the same isLineSpecChar set the .bit
// extractor takes, which means it also runs into the prose that follows a
// citation -- the trailing-separator cases below are the live shapes it must
// give back ("spec/LINT.md:186, E0204)" appears twelve times on 2026-09-09).
// 4012 is runtime/ABI.md's line count that day; the check only has to reject
// an end past it, so nothing here re-reads the file.
fn selfTestMdRangeCitations(): int {
  let failed = 0
  let cites = extractMdCitations("see `runtime/ABI.md:283-290` for the descriptor")
  failed = failed + selfCheck(len(cites) == 1, "did not find the ranged .md citation")
  if (len(cites) == 1) {
    failed = failed +
      selfCheck(cites[0].lineSpecRaw == "283-290", "truncated the ranged .md citation at the dash")
    failed = failed +
      selfCheck(
        segsInBounds(parseSegments(cites[0].lineSpecRaw), 4012),
        "rejected an in-range .md range citation",
      )
  }
  failed = failed +
    selfCheck(
      !segsInBounds(parseSegments("283-99999"), 4012),
      "accepted a .md range whose END is past the cited file's last line",
    )
  // The scan must give back every trailing separator it crossed: a spec that
  // ends in "," or " --" is not parseable and would fail a citation that is
  // in fact correct.
  let trailing = extractMdCitations("(spec/LINT.md:186, E0204) and spec/SPEC.md:68 -- prose")
  failed = failed +
    selfCheck(len(trailing) == 2, "did not find both trailing-punctuation citations")
  if (len(trailing) == 2) {
    failed = failed +
      selfCheck(trailing[0].lineSpecRaw == "186", "swallowed the comma after a .md citation")
    failed = failed +
      selfCheck(
        trailing[1].lineSpecRaw == "68",
        "swallowed the trailing \" --\" after a .md citation",
      )
  }
  // A genuine multi-segment spec still survives, and so does the endOffset a
  // heading annotation is anchored at.
  let multi = extractMdCitations("`docs/development.md:20, 30-31` (\"Some Heading\") states")
  failed = failed + selfCheck(len(multi) == 1, "did not find the multi-segment .md citation")
  if (len(multi) == 1) {
    failed = failed +
      selfCheck(multi[0].lineSpecRaw == "20, 30-31", "misparsed the multi-segment .md citation")
    let (needle, ok) = headingAnnotationAfter(
      "`docs/development.md:20, 30-31` (\"Some Heading\") states",
      multi[0].endOffset,
    )
    failed = failed +
      selfCheck(
        ok && needle == "Some Heading",
        "lost the heading annotation after a wider spec scan",
      )
  }
  return failed
}

// #4670: a `<doc>.md:NNN` citation that drifted to a still-in-range line is
// invisible to a bounds check. The measured instance was
// compiler/arm64compile.bit:83, which named line 1160 of spec/SPEC.md — a
// blank line inside the traits section — for a clobber-list sentence that
// lives at spec/SPEC.md:1414 today; 1160 is well inside 4747 lines, so
// bounds passed it forever. lineHasCitableContent is the floor that catches
// that shape, and the verdicts below are the ones it has to keep apart.
fn selfTestCitableContent(): int {
  let lines = ["## A heading", "", "   ", "real prose here", "```bit", "~~~", "  ``` "]
  let failed = 0
  failed = failed + selfCheck(lineHasCitableContent(lines, 1), "rejected a heading line")
  failed = failed + selfCheck(lineHasCitableContent(lines, 4), "rejected a prose line")
  failed = failed + selfCheck(!lineHasCitableContent(lines, 2), "accepted an empty line")
  failed = failed + selfCheck(!lineHasCitableContent(lines, 3), "accepted a whitespace-only line")
  failed = failed + selfCheck(!lineHasCitableContent(lines, 5), "accepted a ``` fence marker")
  failed = failed + selfCheck(!lineHasCitableContent(lines, 6), "accepted a ~~~ fence marker")
  failed = failed + selfCheck(!lineHasCitableContent(lines, 7), "accepted an indented fence marker")
  failed = failed + selfCheck(!lineHasCitableContent(lines, 0), "accepted line 0")
  failed = failed + selfCheck(!lineHasCitableContent(lines, 8), "accepted a line past the end")
  // A range citation is content-checked at its START, which is the line the
  // spec names first -- specFirstLine is what both arms pass in.
  failed = failed + selfCheck(specFirstLine("283-290") == 283, "did not take a range's start")
  failed = failed + selfCheck(specFirstLine("20, 30-31") == 20, "did not take the first segment")
  failed = failed + selfCheck(specFirstLine("not a spec") == 0, "invented a line for a bad spec")
  return failed
}
